@comment{
====================================================================
DOMAIN: The 1784 "Idea for a Universal History with a Cosmopolitan
        Purpose" -- Text and Interpretive Debates
SEARCH_DATE: 2026-03-13
PAPERS_FOUND: 15 total (High: 8, Medium: 5, Low: 2)
SEARCH_SOURCES: SEP, IEP, PhilPapers, Semantic Scholar, OpenAlex,
                CrossRef (verification)
====================================================================

DOMAIN_OVERVIEW:
The 1784 essay "Idee zu einer allgemeinen Geschichte in
weltbuergerlicher Absicht" occupies a peculiar position in Kant's
corpus: it is neither a work of the critical philosophy proper nor
a mere popular essay, but rather a methodological proposal for
writing universal history "from a cosmopolitan point of view."
Secondary literature has been preoccupied with three interlocking
problems. First, the status of teleology: does the invocation of
"nature's plan" or "nature's cunning" operate as a constitutive
theoretical claim about historical causation, or merely as a
regulative heuristic for the historian? Lindstedt (1999) and
Rauscher (2002) argue for strong regulative readings; Kuehn (2009)
argues the essay is primarily about historiography (Geschichtsschreibung),
not metaphysics of history. Second, the role of "unsocial
sociability" (ungesellige Geselligkeit): Schneewind (2009)
reconstructs it as the engine of moral development -- the mechanism
by which evil serves good -- while Caranti (2015) defends a
naturalized, post-Darwinian reading that decouples the mechanism
from Kant's metaphysics of natural dispositions. Third, the
relation to practical philosophy: Kleingeld (2001) argues that
the philosophy of history is indispensable to Kant's moral theory,
providing the empirical substrate that pure practical reason alone
cannot supply; James (2019) identifies a gap between the causal
mechanism Kant invokes and the moral motivation that the plan of
nature is supposed to produce.

Recent scholarship has moved in two directions. One strand (Lenczewska
2022, 2025; Woo 2023) explores the developmental dimension: how
does the historical emergence and growth of reason connect to
Kant's systematic practical philosophy? Another strand (Kryluk
2024; Williams 2024) examines the regulative-vs-constitutive
distinction with fresh precision, connecting it to Kant's political
writings of the 1790s. The Rorty/Schmidt critical guide (2009)
remains the central reference point for the secondary literature,
collecting essays by Kuehn, Schneewind, O'Neill, Kleingeld, and
others that map the major interpretive positions.

RELEVANCE_TO_PROJECT:
This domain covers the primary text and its immediate interpretive
debates, providing the textual and conceptual foundation for all
other domains in the project. The key question -- whether Kant's
philosophy of history is a theoretical claim about natural causation,
a regulative idea for practical orientation, or a "speculative
narrative" in a looser sense -- determines how the essay relates
to the Groundwork (published the following year), the Critique of
Judgment's teleology, and later writings on history.

NOTABLE_GAPS:
German-language scholarship (Brandt, Klemme, Sturm) on the essay
is underrepresented in English-language databases; the Caranti
paper is in a Brazilian journal and may be less accessible. The
connection between the 1784 essay and Kant's pre-critical universal
natural history (1755) remains understudied. Lindstedt (1999) and
O'Neill (2008) could not have abstracts retrieved from any source.

SYNTHESIS_GUIDANCE:
The regulative/constitutive distinction is the key axis for
synthesis: use it to organize competing readings of nature's
"guarantee" and the epistemic status of Kant's claims about
progress. Connect the mechanism of unsocial sociability to the
practical philosophy sections of the review.

KEY_POSITIONS:
- Regulative/practical reading: 4 papers -- History as idea of
  practical reason, not theoretical metaphysics
- Constitutive/teleological reading: 3 papers -- Nature genuinely
  has a plan that grounds historical explanation
- Historiographical reading: 2 papers -- Essay is about how to
  write history, not about history's deep structure
- Naturalized/reformed reading: 2 papers -- Defending the essay
  by detaching it from objectionable metaphysics
====================================================================
}

@book{rorty2009kant,
  editor = {Rorty, Am{\'e}lie Oksenberg and Schmidt, James},
  title = {Kant's Idea for a Universal History with a Cosmopolitan Aim},
  year = {2009},
  publisher = {Cambridge University Press},
  doi = {10.1017/cbo9780511581434},
  abstract = {Lively debates about narratives of historical progress,
    the conditions for international justice, and the implications
    of globalisation have prompted a renewed interest in Kant's Idea
    for a Universal History with a Cosmopolitan Aim. The essays in
    this volume, written by distinguished contributors, discuss the
    questions that are at the core of Kant's investigations. Does the
    study of history convey any philosophical insight? Can it provide
    political guidance? How are we to understand the destructive and
    bloody upheavals that constitute so much of human experience? What
    connections, if any, can be traced between politics, economics,
    and morality? What is the relation between the rule of law in the
    nation state and the advancement of a cosmopolitan political order?
    These questions and others are examined and discussed in a book
    that will be of interest to philosophers, social and political
    theorists, and intellectual and cultural historians.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: This critical guide collects fourteen essays by
  leading Kant scholars engaging with the 1784 essay from multiple
  angles: its argumentative structure, the role of unsocial
  sociability, the teleological framework, and the relation to
  Kant's practical and political philosophy. The editors' introduction
  frames history-as-philosophy as the organizing theme. Contributors
  include Kuehn, Schneewind, O'Neill, Kleingeld, Wood, and others
  who represent the major interpretive positions in the secondary
  literature.

  RELEVANCE: As the primary locus of contemporary secondary
  literature on the 1784 essay, this volume is indispensable for
  mapping the debate. Individual essays directly address the key
  questions of the research project: the status of teleological
  claims, the role of unsocial sociability, and the relation to
  the critical system.

  POSITION: Reference anthology organizing the field's major
  interpretive positions.
  },
  keywords = {kant-philosophy-of-history, unsocial-sociability,
    teleology, cosmopolitan-aim, critical-guide, High}
}

@incollection{kuehn2009reason,
  author = {Kuehn, Manfred},
  title = {Reason as a species characteristic},
  booktitle = {Kant's Idea for a Universal History with a
    Cosmopolitan Aim},
  editor = {Rorty, Am{\'e}lie Oksenberg and Schmidt, James},
  year = {2009},
  publisher = {Cambridge University Press},
  pages = {68--93},
  doi = {10.1017/cbo9780511581434.005},
  abstract = {Some philosophical scholars understand the Idea for a
    Universal History with a Cosmopolitan Aim of 1784 as Kant's most
    explicit formulation of his "philosophy of history." Put
    differently, they understand this essay as his attempt to uncover
    the fundamental laws of the historical development of humanity,
    similar in intent to the efforts of Georg Wilhelm Friedrich Hegel,
    Karl Marx, and others, who offered compelling accounts of what they
    took to be the meaning and direction of history. But there are many
    reasons why such an interpretation should be rejected. The English
    word "history" is just as ambiguous as the German word
    "Geschichte." It can refer either to the totality of events that
    we call "history" or to the way in which history is conceived or
    written. The Universal History presents and defends an "idea" and a
    "point of view" for the writing of such a universal or world
    history. In other words, Kant was addressing in this essay
    Geschichtsschreibung or historiography, advocating and giving
    reasons for a certain way of writing history from a philosophical
    point of view that introduces a "plan" or a "purpose of nature"
    into the historical account we give of human events.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Kuehn argues against reading the 1784 essay as
  Kant's version of a Hegelian-style metaphysics of history. The
  essay is primarily a proposal about historiography
  (Geschichtsschreibung) -- a methodological argument for how to
  write universal history from a philosophical point of view that
  introduces a "plan" or "purpose of nature" as an organizing
  heuristic. This reading deflates the metaphysical ambitions that
  some interpret in the text and aligns it with the regulative use
  of reason.

  RELEVANCE: Directly addresses the key question of whether the
  essay makes constitutive or merely regulative claims, situating
  it as a methodological rather than metaphysical text. Challenges
  interpretations that place it on a par with Hegel or Marx as a
  "philosophy of history" in the strong sense.

  POSITION: Historiographical/deflationary reading; regulative
  use of teleological ideas.
  },
  keywords = {kant-philosophy-of-history, historiography,
    regulative-idea, teleology, High}
}

@incollection{schneewind2009good,
  author = {Schneewind, J. B.},
  title = {Good out of evil: {Kant} and the idea of unsocial
    sociability},
  booktitle = {Kant's Idea for a Universal History with a
    Cosmopolitan Aim},
  editor = {Rorty, Am{\'e}lie Oksenberg and Schmidt, James},
  year = {2009},
  publisher = {Cambridge University Press},
  pages = {94--111},
  doi = {10.1017/cbo9780511581434.006},
  abstract = {There is nothing so unsociable and so sociable as man:
    the one by his vice, the other by his nature. (Montaigne)},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Schneewind reconstructs "unsocial sociability"
  as the philosophical heart of the 1784 essay: the mechanism by
  which nature converts human vice and antagonism into the
  conditions for moral and political development. He situates this
  within the broader tradition of theodicy -- the problem of
  justifying an apparently malign natural order -- and argues that
  Kant transforms the problem into a philosophical account of
  progress that requires neither divine intervention nor optimistic
  assumptions about human nature.

  RELEVANCE: This essay directly addresses one of the project's
  key questions: what philosophical work does "unsocial sociability"
  do? Schneewind's theodicy framing connects the 1784 essay to
  the broader question of whether nature "guarantees" progress,
  which is central to the research project.

  POSITION: Theodicy/teleological reading; unsocial sociability
  as genuine philosophical principle.
  },
  keywords = {kant-philosophy-of-history, unsocial-sociability,
    theodicy, nature-cunning, High}
}

@article{kleingeld2001nature,
  author = {Kleingeld, Pauline},
  title = {Nature or {Providence}? {On} the Theoretical and Moral
    Importance of {Kant}'s Philosophy of History},
  journal = {American Catholic Philosophical Quarterly},
  year = {2001},
  volume = {75},
  number = {2},
  pages = {201--219},
  doi = {10.5840/acpq200175216},
  abstract = {Soulevant la question de la relation entre nature et
    providence dans la philosophie de l'histoire kantienne, l'A.
    montre que la preference de Kant pour le terme de providence dans
    L'idee d'une histoire universelle correspond au passage de la
    question theorique de l'ordre dans l'histoire a une approche
    morale de l'histoire fondee sur le principe teleologique.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Kleingeld argues that Kant's philosophy of history
  is not a peripheral or merely popular supplement to the critical
  system but is theoretically and morally indispensable. She
  distinguishes Kant's use of "nature" from "providence" and
  argues that the historical teleology provides the empirical
  substrate needed to make practical moral hope rational: without
  the claim that nature works in history toward cosmopolitan
  conditions, the moral demand to work for such conditions lacks
  the kind of empirical support that makes it more than a mere
  wish.

  RELEVANCE: Directly addresses the relation between the 1784
  essay and practical philosophy, which is a central question of
  the research project. Kleingeld's argument that the philosophy
  of history is morally indispensable frames the debate about
  whether the essay is merely "empirical/pragmatic" or
  "normative/transcendental."

  POSITION: Strong integration thesis; philosophy of history as
  necessary to practical philosophy.
  },
  keywords = {kant-philosophy-of-history, nature-providence,
    practical-philosophy, cosmopolitan-aim, High}
}

@article{lindstedt1999progress,
  author = {Lindstedt, David},
  title = {Kant: {Progress} in Universal History as a Postulate
    of Practical Reason},
  journal = {Kant-Studien},
  year = {1999},
  volume = {90},
  number = {2},
  pages = {129--147},
  doi = {10.1515/kant.1999.90.2.129},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Lindstedt argues that historical progress in
  Kant's philosophy should be understood on the model of the
  postulates of practical reason: not as a theoretical claim
  knowable a priori or confirmed empirically, but as a
  practically-necessary assumption that rational agents must
  make in order to act effectively toward moral ends. This reading
  places the philosophy of history firmly on the practical side
  of the theoretical/practical divide.

  RELEVANCE: Provides a precise account of the epistemic status
  of Kant's claim that nature "guarantees" progress, one of the
  project's key questions. The postulate reading is a significant
  alternative to both constitutive-theoretical and merely
  heuristic-regulative interpretations.

  POSITION: Practical-postulate reading; progress as normatively
  required assumption, not theoretical knowledge.
  },
  keywords = {kant-philosophy-of-history, progress, practical-postulate,
    regulative-idea, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@article{rauscher2002regulative,
  author = {Rauscher, Frederick},
  title = {The Regulative and the Constitutive {In Kant's} and
    {Hegel's} Theories of History},
  journal = {Idealistic Studies},
  year = {2002},
  volume = {32},
  number = {2},
  pages = {121--142},
  doi = {10.5840/idstudies20023229},
  abstract = {I show one reason why Hegel's theory of history is an
    improvement over Kant's. There is an ambiguity in Kant's theory
    of history. He wants, on the one hand, to distinguish empirical
    history from reason's a priori regulative role in theory. On the
    other hand, his view of the nature of sciences and the role of
    reason precludes such a separation. I trace this problem to
    different roles assigned the faculties of understanding and reason
    in our experience. In Hegel's theory of history, both reason and
    understanding together constitute the sciences, and thus
    experience. Hegel argues that history is a unified field employing
    both understanding and reason. I conclude that the more consistent
    theory of history for idealists is Hegel's, and that this
    consistency partially explains the movement in German Idealism from
    Kantian to Hegelian thought.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Rauscher examines the distinction between
  regulative and constitutive uses of teleological ideas in
  Kant's (and Hegel's) theories of history. He argues that for
  Kant, the idea of historical progress is genuinely regulative:
  it guides the investigation and interpretation of historical
  events without asserting that the pattern exists independently
  of the inquiry. This is contrasted with Hegel's constitutive
  teleology, which asserts that Spirit actually determines
  historical outcomes. Rauscher's analysis illuminates how the
  regulative/constitutive distinction cuts across the Kant/Hegel
  divide.

  RELEVANCE: Directly addresses the key question of whether
  Kant's universal history is a theoretical claim about natural
  teleology or a regulative idea for practical orientation. The
  Kant/Hegel comparison is also relevant to the project's domain
  on the development of Kant's philosophy of history in relation
  to successors.

  POSITION: Regulative reading; contrasted with Hegelian
  constitutive teleology.
  },
  keywords = {kant-philosophy-of-history, regulative-constitutive,
    teleology, hegel-comparison, High}
}

@article{james2019practical,
  author = {James, David},
  title = {Practical Necessity and the Fulfilment of the Plan of
    Nature in {Kant}'s Idea for a Universal History},
  journal = {Journal of the Philosophy of History},
  year = {2019},
  volume = {13},
  number = {1},
  pages = {42--65},
  doi = {10.1163/18722636-12341361},
  abstract = {I explore the role of practical necessity in Kant's
    essay Idea for a Universal History with a Cosmopolitan Aim. This
    form of necessity arises on the basis of social and interstate
    antagonism and Kant appeals to it with the aim of avoiding the
    introduction of a standpoint that is external to the agents whose
    attitudes and actions are being described. In connection with the
    role that Kant accords to practical necessity in the establishment
    of the legal and political conditions required to fulfil the
    "plan of nature" in the course of history, I argue that in this
    essay he fails to identify a mechanism that would explain a
    fundamental moral change in that which motivates human beings once
    these conditions have been established. This in turn invites
    questions concerning the kind of universal history that Kant
    proposes. In particular, I argue that the choice of historical
    material that it demands could, in certain circumstances, be
    regarded as counterpurposive in relation to the aim of nature of
    which Kant speaks in the same essay.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: James reconstructs the role of "practical
  necessity" in the 1784 essay: Kant appeals to social and
  interstate antagonism as a form of practical compulsion that
  drives agents toward legal and political arrangements without
  assuming external divine intervention or purely voluntary moral
  conversion. James argues, however, that the essay fails to
  identify a mechanism explaining how agents undergo the
  fundamental moral change required once legal conditions are
  established, creating a gap between causal-mechanical and
  moral-motivational levels of the argument.

  RELEVANCE: Identifies a specific structural problem in the
  1784 essay's argument -- the gap between the mechanism of
  unsocial sociability and the moral outcome it is supposed to
  produce -- that is directly relevant to the project's question
  about whether the essay is primarily empirical/pragmatic or
  normative/transcendental.

  POSITION: Critical/analytical reading; identifies an
  argumentative lacuna in the mechanical-to-moral transition.
  },
  keywords = {kant-philosophy-of-history, practical-necessity,
    plan-of-nature, unsocial-sociability, High}
}

@article{caranti2015defending,
  author = {Caranti, Luigi},
  title = {Defending {Kant} after {Darwin}: a Reassessment of
    Idea for a Universal History with a Cosmopolitan Purpose},
  journal = {Estudos Kantianos},
  year = {2015},
  volume = {3},
  number = {01},
  doi = {10.36311/2318-0501/2015.v3n01.5120},
  abstract = {The paper argues that Kant's teleology in Idea for a
    Universal History with a Cosmopolitan Purpose can be salvaged
    only if the mechanism of social unsociability, considered as the
    true center of the essay, is (a) detached from the hardly
    defensible notion of natural dispositions and (b) understood in
    conjunction with general premises that Kant does not make
    explicit, but rather takes as self-evidently true. In this
    perspective, Kant's teleology is reduced to the affirmation that,
    given certain constant features of human beings (mainly, limited
    benevolence and ability to see their best interest through
    experience) as well as relatively constant objective circumstances
    of the world we live in, an approximation of human affairs towards
    the cosmopolitan constitution is the most likely outcome. The paper
    moves the first steps towards a defense of this thesis by
    reformulating Kant's argument in a way to make it compatible with
    contemporary science.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Caranti argues that Kant's teleology in the
  1784 essay can be salvaged if unsocial sociability is
  (a) detached from the metaphysically objectionable notion of
  "natural dispositions" and (b) reconstructed in terms of
  relatively constant features of human nature (limited
  benevolence, ability to learn from experience) and objective
  circumstances (finite resources, competitive interdependence).
  On this naturalized reading, approximation toward cosmopolitan
  constitutions becomes the most likely outcome without requiring
  commitment to teleological metaphysics.

  RELEVANCE: Offers a post-Darwinian defense of the 1784 essay's
  central mechanism, addressing the question of whether the
  argument requires objectionable natural teleology or can be
  reformulated in more defensible terms. Directly relevant to
  the project's question about the philosophical status of
  "nature's cunning."

  POSITION: Reformist/naturalized reading; defends the
  essay by detaching it from pre-critical metaphysics.
  },
  keywords = {kant-philosophy-of-history, unsocial-sociability,
    naturalism, teleology, darwin, Medium}
}

@article{oneill2008historical,
  author = {O'Neill, Onora},
  title = {Historical trends and human futures},
  journal = {Studies in History and Philosophy of Science Part A},
  year = {2008},
  volume = {39},
  number = {4},
  pages = {529--534},
  doi = {10.1016/j.shpsa.2008.09.005},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: O'Neill argues that Kant's 1784 essay differs
  fundamentally from standard Enlightenment views of history:
  human progress is better understood as a practical assumption
  -- an Idea of Reason -- than as a theoretical prediction or
  empirical hypothesis. She distinguishes Kant's position from
  both optimistic progressivism and pessimism by showing that the
  "cosmopolitan aim" is a practical orientation for action, not
  a forecast about future events.

  RELEVANCE: Provides a clear statement of the practical/regulative
  reading of the 1784 essay, directly addressing the project's
  question about the epistemic status of the claim that nature
  "guarantees" progress. O'Neill's framing in terms of "Ideas of
  Reason" connects the essay to the broader critical philosophy.

  POSITION: Practical/regulative reading; history as Idea of
  Reason, not theoretical claim.
  },
  keywords = {kant-philosophy-of-history, practical-orientation,
    idea-of-reason, progress, Medium, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@article{woo2023hope,
  author = {Woo, Jaeha},
  title = {Why {Kant}'s Hope Took a Historical Turn in Practical
    Philosophy},
  journal = {Con-Textos Kantianos. International Journal of
    Philosophy},
  year = {2023},
  volume = {17},
  pages = {43--55},
  doi = {10.5209/kant.88695},
  abstract = {In the beginning of his critical period, Kant treated
    the perfect attainment of the highest good -- the unconditioned
    totality of ends which would uphold the perfect proportionality
    between moral virtue and happiness -- as both the ground of hope
    for deserved happiness and the final end of our moral life. But
    I argue that Kant moved in the direction of de-emphasizing the
    latter aspect of the highest good, not because it is inappropriate
    or impossible for us to promote this ideal, but because the
    endless pursuit of it offers no prospect of moral satisfaction. I
    take this change as one possible reason for him to shift his focus
    more toward social and political progress in history, which has as
    its main subject the human species which is in some sense
    immortal.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Woo argues that Kant made a systematic shift
  in his critical period from locating moral hope in the perfect
  attainment of the highest good (the proportionality of virtue
  and happiness for individuals) to placing it in social and
  political progress in history, understood as concerning the
  human species rather than individual agents. The shift is
  explained not as a change of subject matter but as a response
  to the problem that pursuit of the individual highest good
  offers "no prospect of moral satisfaction" in this life.

  RELEVANCE: Connects the 1784 essay to Kant's systematic
  practical philosophy by explaining why history acquires moral
  importance for Kant, and how the essay's "cosmopolitan aim"
  functions within the broader architecture of practical reason.
  Addresses the project's question about the relation between
  the essay and the Groundwork.

  POSITION: Developmental/systematic reading; history as locus
  of moral hope displaced from eschatology.
  },
  keywords = {kant-philosophy-of-history, practical-philosophy,
    highest-good, moral-hope, Medium}
}

@article{sargentis2015crisis,
  author = {Sargentis, Konstantinos},
  title = {Crisis, Evil, and Progress in {Kant}'s Philosophy of
    History},
  journal = {Journal of the Philosophy of History},
  year = {2015},
  volume = {9},
  number = {1},
  pages = {71--96},
  doi = {10.1163/18722636-12341292},
  abstract = {The significance of the regulative function of the
    reflecting power of judgment for Kant's philosophy of history is
    widely accepted in the relevant literature. However, less
    attention has been paid to particular modes of reflection with
    reference to history. In the last paragraphs of the Critique of
    the Power of Judgment we find a distinction between the
    "theoretically reflecting power of judgment" and the reflecting
    power of judgment "in accordance with concepts of practical
    reason". In the present paper, I attempt to stress the importance
    of this distinction for Kant's philosophy of history. More
    specifically, it is my view that this distinction leads Kant to a
    double perspective on history, by means of which one can explain
    why the notions of Nature and Providence cannot be used
    interchangeably. Interestingly, at the same time, it facilitates
    a new understanding of what Kant in Religion within the Boundaries
    of mere Reason calls "revolution in the disposition of the human
    being".},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Sargentis examines the role of evil and crisis
  in Kant's philosophy of history, arguing that Kant's account
  of progress is not linear or optimistic but fundamentally
  dialectical: crisis and the experience of radical evil are
  conditions of moral progress rather than obstacles to it.
  Sargentis connects this to the mechanism of unsocial
  sociability and to Kant's anthropology of radical evil
  in Religion. He draws on the distinction between theoretically
  and practically reflecting judgment from the third Critique to
  explain why "nature" and "providence" are not interchangeable.

  RELEVANCE: Provides a reading of the 1784 essay that integrates
  it with Kant's later accounts of radical evil, connecting the
  essay's mechanism to the moral anthropology that the project's
  broader literature review will address. Addresses the question
  of whether Kant's teleology is naively optimistic.

  POSITION: Dialectical/anti-optimistic reading; crisis as
  condition of progress.
  },
  keywords = {kant-philosophy-of-history, evil, progress,
    moral-anthropology, Medium}
}

@article{kryluk2024revolution,
  author = {Kryluk, Michael},
  title = {Between Revolution and Reaction: {The} Political
    Significance of {Kant}'s Doctrine of the Idea},
  journal = {Archiv f{\"u}r Geschichte der Philosophie},
  year = {2024},
  volume = {107},
  number = {2},
  pages = {293--330},
  doi = {10.1515/agph-2023-0093},
  abstract = {This essay argues that Kant's conception of regulative
    ideas of practical reason introduced in the Critique of Pure
    Reason serves an important twofold function in his political
    philosophy. First, Kant's version of the ideal, Platonic republic
    acts as the a priori paradigm of a rightful state to which
    existing regimes can and should conform. Second, Kant frames the
    regulative status of such practical ideas as a resolution of the
    conflict between the extremes of dogmatism and skepticism. In his
    principal political writings from the 1790s -- "Theory and
    Practice," "Perpetual Peace," and the Doctrine of Right -- Kant
    draws on his account of practical ideas in the Critique to
    articulate a counterfactual norm of popular sovereignty that
    distinguishes his political standpoint from opponents on the left
    and the right.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Kryluk argues that the regulative ideas of
  practical reason introduced in the first Critique serve a
  twofold political function in Kant's practical philosophy:
  they provide (a) an a priori paradigm of a rightful state
  against which existing regimes are measured, and (b) a
  resolution to the conflict between political dogmatism
  (revolution as legitimate when ideals are immediately
  realizable) and political skepticism (reform is impossible).
  The doctrine of the regulative idea thus grounds Kant's
  gradualist politics of non-violent reform.

  RELEVANCE: Directly addresses the question of what political
  work regulative ideas do in Kant's practical philosophy.
  The connection between the CPR's doctrine of regulative ideas
  and the political writings (including the 1784 essay) is
  central to the project's question about the relation between
  the essay and the emerging critical project.

  POSITION: Political-regulative reading; connects first Critique
  regulative ideas to historical/political writings.
  },
  keywords = {kant-philosophy-of-history, regulative-idea,
